# Line endings are normalized so a Windows checkout cannot break the toolchain.
#
# Why this file exists, measured rather than assumed: git on Windows defaults to
# core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites LF to CRLF ON CHECKOUT. A bash script with CRLF
# line endings does not run at all:
#   set -uo pipefail   ->  "set: pipefail: invalid option name"
#   case "$v" in       ->  "syntax error near unexpected token `in'"
# So without this file, a Windows clone produced a repository where nothing executed, and
# the failure looked like a broken product rather than a line-ending setting.
#
# Enforced by tests/line-endings.test.sh, so this is a checked rule and not a wish.
* text=auto

# Everything the toolchain executes or the gates parse stays LF on every platform.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
# .ps1 is pinned to LF deliberately, though CRLF is the Windows convention. PowerShell
# parses LF-only scripts correctly, while a CRLF pin would make install.ps1 a permanent
# offender in the tracked-CRLF scan in tests/line-endings.test.sh on every non-Windows
# checkout. LF costs nothing here and keeps one rule instead of a rule plus an exception.
*.ps1   text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
LICENSE text eol=lf
.gitignore     text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf

# `* text=auto` above lets git AUTO-DETECT binaries and leave them alone, so nothing here
# mangles one. There are no binary files in this repo today; if one is added (an image, a
# font, an archive), give it an explicit `-text` line so no future change can normalize it.
